BEGIN_PGML
Recall the average rate of change of a function [`f(x)`] on an interval [`[a,b]`] is

[```AV_{[a,b]}=\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}\text{.}```]

Determine the average rate of [`T`] on the interval [`[60,65]\text{,}`] [`AV_{[60, 65]}\text{.}`]

[_]{$answer3}{10} [_]{$popup2}{5}

END_PGML

Section::End();

############################################################
# Section
############################################################
Section::Begin("");

BEGIN_PGML
The sign of [`AV_{[60, 65]}`] is [_]{$popup}{5}

This sign makes sense because

[_]{$radio3}{5}

END_PGML

Section::End();

############################################################
# Section
############################################################
Section::Begin("");

BEGIN_PGML
Which of the following explains the meaning of the value of [`AV_{[60, 65]}\text{?}`]

[_]{$radio4}{5}

END_PGML

Section::End();

Scaffold::End();

############################################################
# End Problem
############################################################

ENDDOCUMENT();

static.solution:
v(t) = \lim_{h\to0} \frac{5\mathopen{}\left(t+h\right)^{2}+5 - 5t^{2}+5}{h}
        = \lim_{h\to0} \frac{5t^{2}+10th+5h^{2}+5-5t^{2}-5}{h}
        = \lim_{h\to0} 10t+5h = 10 t
and
v(t) = \lim_{h\to0} \frac{10\mathopen{}\left(t+h\right) - 10t}{h}
        = \lim_{h\to0} \frac{10t+10h-10t}{h}
        = \lim_{h\to0} 10 = 10.

statement.p:
For the function
f(x) = \begin{cases} x^2 - 4, \amp  0\le x \lt  2\\ 4, \amp  x = 2\\ 4 x  - 8, \amp  2 \lt  x \end{cases}
use algebra to find each of the following limits:

solution.p:
For small values of h, 
 f'(c) \approx \frac{f(c+h) - f(c)}{h}.
In this problem, c = 4.9, and h = 5.2 - 4.9 = 0.3.
\displaystyle{  f'(4.9) \approx \frac{f(5.2) - f(4.9)}{0.3}
  = \frac{5.9  + 1}{0.3}
  = 23.
}

The function \displaystyle y=\frac{6x}{9x+2} is a quotient of two functions, say f(x)= 6x and g(x)=9x+2.  Then taking the derivatives we get f'(x)=6 and g'(x)=9.  Finally, the quotient rule says that 
\left[\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\right]'=\frac{g(x)f'(x)-f(x)g'(x)}{\left[g(x)\right]^2}
so y'= \frac{(9x+2) \cdot (6)  - (6x)\cdot (9)}{(9x+2)^2}= \frac{12}{(9 x + 2)^2}.

The function \displaystyle y=\frac{x^{7}}{\sqrt{x}+7x^{6}} is a quotient of two functions, say f(x)= x^{7} and g(x)=\sqrt{x}+7x^{6}.  Then taking the derivatives we get f'(x)=7x^{6} and g'(x)=\frac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}+42x^{5}.  Finally, the quotient rule says that 
\left[\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\right]'=\frac{g(x)f'(x)-f(x)g'(x)}{\left[g(x)\right]^2}
so y'= \frac{(\sqrt{x}+7x^{6}) \cdot (7x^{6})  - (x^{7})\cdot \frac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}+42x^{5}}{(\sqrt{x}+7x^{6})^2}.

